The right approach is: study this guide deeply for one DGX node, then learn how to scale that node into an AI cluster.
Recommended study sequence: spend about 18–22 focused hours on it across four weeks.
1. Week 1 — DGX/HGX architecture and networking
   Read guide pages 3–15 (PDF pages 9–21). Learn the 8-GPU architecture, NVLink/NVSwitch, ConnectX-7 networking, management interfaces, storage, PSU redundancy, and network-port mapping.
   Deliverable: create a one-page “DGX H100 Node Architecture” diagram and explain the traffic separation: BMC/OOB, management, storage, and InfiniBand compute fabric.
2. Week 2 — First boot and BMC operations
   Read pages 17–26 and 39–51 (PDF pages 23–32 and 45–57). Focus on BMC access, isolated management network/VLAN, console access, initial OS/network setup, RAID rebuild awareness, authentication, certificates, and event monitoring.
   Deliverable: write DGX-Day0-Day1-Runbook.md in your GitHub portfolio:
   - Rack and cable acceptance
   - BMC IP configuration and secure access
   - First boot
   - Firmware/version collection
   - OS and network validation
   - Escalation criteria
3. Week 3 — GPU health validation and container readiness
   Read pages 27–34 (PDF pages 33–40). This includes nvsm show health, pre-flight stress tests, Docker validation, and running nvidia-smi inside an NVIDIA CUDA container.
   Practice this on a Lambda Cloud GPU instance once you have your basic Docker/Kubernetes foundation. This is the first place where Lambda Labs adds real value.
   Deliverable: save sanitized command output and write gpu-node-validation.md:
   - nvidia-smi
   - GPU inventory
   - Docker GPU runtime test
   - CUDA container test
   - Health-check interpretation
   - Fault/alert decision tree
4. Week 4 — Power, Redfish, firmware, and troubleshooting
   Read pages 53–89 (PDF pages 59–95). Focus on power capping, redundancy, Redfish inventory, firmware lifecycle, BIOS settings, thermal/power telemetry, SEL/event logs, and read-only troubleshooting.
   Deliverable: create a Redfish-and-Firmware-Operations.md runbook. Do not memorize every API command; understand the workflow: inventory → assess health → approve change → firmware update → reboot → validate → document rollback path.
Skip or only skim:
- Compliance, notices, and legal sections
- Detailed physical part numbers
- Exact cable ordering details beyond knowing how to validate cables/transceivers
- Deep directory-service integration details unless you are asked about enterprise BMC integration
